---
import UtilityBelonging from "../../../components/studies/UtilityBelonging.svelte";
import UtilityLanguage from "../../../components/studies/UtilityLanguage.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "ship-utility-language-2026-11-02",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <aside aria-label="Study closure">
    <h2>Closed / Failed study</h2>
    <p>
      Closed on 2 November 2026 by explicit designer instruction: “the study
      completely and utterly failed.” No visual direction was accepted. The
      examples remain as evidence, including the superseded opening comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      The designer reports that successive course corrections moved the work
      further from the intended visual identity, and sees a similar pattern
      after 02.4 in the previous study. This is an attributed interpretation;
      the cause has not been established. Closure is a valid outcome, separate
      from design acceptance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Evidence record:
      <code
        >docs/loop-evidence/2026-11-02-ship-utility-language-failed-study.md</code
      >. Readiness and scope were not reached. Discussion of a future prompt is
      separate from this closed cycle.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Machinery that belongs with the ship</h2>
    <p>
      Void Drift already has a visual language for ships and engines: deliberate
      silhouettes, solid low-poly parts, flat lit planes and restrained
      contrast. Utilities need a similarly convincing physical presence. The
      first question is how to build devices that look good alongside those
      established objects and feel like machinery from the same world.
    </p>
    <p>
      A fitted ship should visibly carry a substantial, purposeful device. The
      immediate investigation is its aesthetic relationship to the hull and
      engines: material, mass, construction and contrast. Identifying a device's
      exact function or subtype is a later concern, not the entry requirement.
      Storage, solar charging, gravity charging and automatic repair remain the
      joint test set so the emerging direction is not tailored to one attractive
      battery.
    </p>
    <p>
      Machinery stays exposed and deliberately engineered. Storage holds energy
      in surfaces, lattices or fields; it does not animate or disclose charge.
      Chassis storage remains invisible. Solar or gravity charging depends on
      ship type. Color is unresolved, with orange excluded for storage. Bulky
      equipment may change a ship's character. Production implementation and
      numerical balance remain outside this study.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Exploration</h2>
    <h3>02.1 / Working machinery against the established hull</h3>
    <p>
      One exposed construction family, in two material treatments. Broad faceted
      solids, recessed working surfaces, substantial connectors and open gaps
      carry the objects. A uses pale alloy with dark working surfaces; B holds
      the geometry fixed and tests graphite alloy with muted yellow surfaces.
      Color is a material proposal, never a charge indicator.
    </p>
    <p>
      In each column: enlarged device, the identical device on the current Lance
      with its production Light ion engines, then an unfitted/fitted pair at
      0.65 flight scale. The question is whether the equipment feels substantial
      and native to this ship, and whether it visibly adds a device at flight
      size. Function recognition and variants are not being decided here.
    </p>
    <UtilityBelonging client:load />
    <p>
      Mounting: actual hull-0 equipment mount at local (2, 0), nominal 11 × 9.
      Positions and engine definitions are unchanged. Proposed device bodies
      span roughly 16–20 units and overhang that footprint; this is an explicit
      bulky-equipment proposal, not validated clearance. Views share orientation
      and upper-left lighting. All details are scalable polygon construction; no
      animation or additional detail appears on enlargement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reference retained: earlier study 02.3–02.4 supplied useful evidence of
      faceted volume, separated working parts and exposed connections. These new
      constructions investigate that substance across all four roles; they do
      not adopt the earlier vessels as storage technology. A single hull and
      static views cannot establish compatibility across the fleet or
      readability in motion.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Superseded opening comparison and recovery notes</summary>

      <p>
        The first comparison did not provide a useful basis for judgment: the
        devices felt unrelated to the game, were hard to read, and lacked
        convincing machinery. Neither candidate is a direction to refine by
        default.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary>
          Initial comparison / Industrial machinery or scientific apparatus? —
          superseded
        </summary>
        <p>
          Two construction hypotheses share the same muted yellow-green material
          palette so color does not decide the comparison. Both expose their
          working parts. Industrial banks emphasize sturdy bearers and
          repetition; scientific assemblies emphasize separated elements and
          precise supports. Neither is an accepted direction.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each column uses the current Lance fighter and its two Light ion
          engines, with the candidate at the actual hull-0 equipment mount (2,
          0; nominal 11 × 9). No mount is relocated. Solar collectors and some
          apparatus overhang that footprint: proposed equipment envelopes, not a
          change to slot positions. Each role is fitted separately for a
          controlled comparison; this is not a proposed four-device loadout.
        </p>
        <UtilityLanguage client:load />
        <p>
          Top: actual 0.65 flight scale. Middle: the identical ship and object
          enlarged, with light from the upper left. Bottom: the same object and
          a repeated-element variant probe. Gravity and repair currently repeat
          unchanged in the variant position: their subtype distinction remains
          unproven. All views are static. This tests impression and silhouette,
          not animation, motion readability or sixty-component coverage.
        </p>
        <p>
          The small views are the demanding evidence: if only the enlarged
          apparatus distinguishes a function, the language needs revision.
          Storage uses repeated surfaces, solar uses spread collectors, gravity
          uses opposing masses or a segmented field assembly, and repair uses
          paired service arms. The open question is whether those differences
          survive as members of one recognizable family.
        </p>
      </details>
      <h3>Visual foundation / what the earlier exploration made discussable</h3>
      <p>
        The designer identifies the previous study through 02.4 as useful
        exploration despite its eventual failure. It is consulted here
        specifically to understand that usefulness; it remains closed and
        establishes no accepted utility design. In its 02.3, exposed cells and
        clamps could be compared with a surrounding cradle. In 02.4, vessels, a
        helix, separated discs and connected assemblies offered distinct
        physical identities within a shared faceted material treatment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those objects gave concrete impressions to respond to: contained volume,
        winding, layered working surfaces, and connected major and minor parts.
        The current comparison reduced construction largely to bars with bright
        edges. The recovery should preserve the earlier examples as visual
        references for substance and legible assembly, without adopting their
        battery technology or copying their shapes across every function. All
        four utilities remain the subject of this cycle.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reference:
        <a href="/lab/studies/utilities-2026-11-02"
          >closed utilities study, 02.3–02.4</a
        >. The next visual investigation remains undeveloped; this records the
        corrected purpose, not a new candidate or design acceptance.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section><h2>03–04 / Not reached</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>
